WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.130
An ambitious idea that spent most of a decade
in a cycle of living and dying might have

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.793
reset the clock on itself for the last time.

00:00:07.793 --> 00:00:12.094
Here's why the Edge of Tomorrow sequel may
be buried in the rubble of yesterday.

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:17.510
While speaking with MTV News in 2015, Tom
Cruise revealed that he had pitched an idea

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:22.570
for an "Edge of Tomorrow" sequel to director
Doug Liman and co-writer Christopher McQuarrie.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.080
Though the star didn't reveal any details,
Cruise confirmed that Blunt would be interested

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.830
in joining the project the following year.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.800
A few months after Cruise's comments, McQuarrie
confirmed to Collider that the creative team

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.820
had the idea for the sequel ready to go.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.850
Ideas turned into words as 2016 brought the
news that "Race" writers Joe Shrapnel and

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.570
Anna Waterhouse would be penning the sequel,
with Deadline reporting that McQuarrie would

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.190
join as producer and script consultant.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:54.010
By the end of 2016, Liman had signed on as
director and revealed to Collider that the

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.300
upcoming sequel would be a prequel.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.420
It's unclear if that was the idea Cruise originally
had pitched.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.269
Liman, however, was completely enthralled
by the idea of returning to the world of "Edge

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:05.920
of Tomorrow," telling IGN,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.490
With such high expectations, one would assume
that cameras would begin rolling as soon as

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:28.335
possible, but Cruise instead went on to focus
on "Mission: Impossible — Fallout," stalling momentum.

00:01:28.335 --> 00:01:33.400
As 2018 came around, it was confirmed that
the sequel would be titled "Live Die Repeat

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:39.320
and Repeat," with Liman enthusiastically telling
Collider that he, Blunt, and Cruise were passionate

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.590
about the sequel.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:45.300
He also confirmed that previous scheduling
issues between the trio had been worked out.

00:01:45.698 --> 00:01:49.759
While Doug Liman had hopes to film the "Edge
of Tomorrow" sequel after "Chaos Walking,"

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:51.790
the film is still in pre-production.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.468
Will "Live Die Repeat and Repeat" ever come
out?

00:01:54.468 --> 00:01:55.798
It seems unlikely.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:01.250
A few short months after Liman expressed enthusiasm
about Tom Cruise and Emily Blunt returning,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.780
the director revealed that a new script was
being written by Jez Butterworth, who co-wrote

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.560
the original film, via Collider.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.650
Later, "The Invention of Lying" writer Matthew
Robinson was courted in 2019 to rewrite the

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.280
script, The Hollywood Reporter confirmed.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.739
While the "Live Die Repeat and Repeat" script
was changing hands, Blunt revealed to IndieWire

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.960
that Cruise wanted the sequel to film in 2017.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.108
The actress declined as she had already signed
on as the titular character in "Mary Poppins Returns."

00:02:28.108 --> 00:02:32.739
Blunt did, however, confirm that was interested
in returning and was hopeful that Liman would

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:34.319
produce a quality script.

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:39.300
And the director did, confirming in 2019 that
the script was ready, putting the ball in

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.980
Cruise and Blunt's court, via ComicBookMovie.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.360
In 2021, Liman told Collider that the script
was still being worked on but that it was

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.810
in a good state.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.980
Revealing that it didn't matter how far along
the script was, he said,

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:01.250
With Christopher McQuarrie and a wide variety
of other writers working on the project, changing

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:06.595
its vision, it's easy to see why Cruise and
Blunt didn't commit early on in the writing process.

00:03:06.654 --> 00:03:11.057
"Can't stand the sight of blood, not so much
as a papercut." [laughing]

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.650
Ask Emily Blunt and she'll say that the proposed
sequel has too large a price tag.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:19.959
Speaking to Howard Stern in 2021, the actress
said she can't imagine how the film would

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:21.750
be made in a post-COVID world.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.699
"Honestly, I think the movie's too expensive."

00:03:23.699 --> 00:03:28.159
Following the rising cost of goods and supply
chain issues for key products, Hollywood has

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:33.010
been actively trying to lower production costs,
according to The Hollywood Reporter.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.010
Blunt said,

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:50.540
Warner Bros., the studio behind "Edge of Tomorrow,"
has seen its fair share of difficulties in

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:55.260
recent years, casting doubts on their ability
to bankroll a less-than-sure project.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:02.540
After AT&amp;T exited the company, the entertainment
giant holds $47.5 billion in net debt, according

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:07.875
to Insider Intelligence, making it crucial
that the next few years feature a string of hits.

00:04:07.948 --> 00:04:12.790
Even if Cruise, Blunt, and Liman could align
their schedules, it doesn't look like Warner

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.350
Bros. is in the position to pony up the $100
million plus the "Edge of Tomorrow" sequel

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:18.350
would demand.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.710
However, fans of the franchise shouldn't lose
hope, as there's a possibility that a series

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.976
could hit HBO Max in the coming years.

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:27.382
"You're not going to believe what I'm about
to tell you."

00:04:27.382 --> 00:04:28.522
"No, I'm not."

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:35.010
Warner Bros. made the shocking choice in 2021
to send its entire theatrical slate to its

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.050
newly launched streaming HBO Max.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:41.780
Village Roadshow, a frequent co-financier
of Warner Bros., sued the media giant for

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:47.643
a breach of contract by sending "The Matrix
Resurrections" to HBO Max, according to The Guardian.

00:04:47.643 --> 00:04:52.620
Made publicly available in 2022, the court
documents of the lawsuit revealed that "Edge

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:56.960
of Tomorrow," another property that Village
Roadshow had its hands in developing, is on

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.259
track to receive the HBO Max treatment.

00:04:59.259 --> 00:05:03.270
The contents of the documents don't suggest
what stage of development the "Edge of Tomorrow"

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.470
series is in, or if it will feature Tom Cruise
and Emily Blunt.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:12.060
No creatives, like Doug Liman or Christopher
McQuarrie are publicly attached as of now.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.260
Recent years have seen several successful
films be adapted for television, like "Let

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.169
The Right One In" and "Cobra Kai."

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:23.139
So it's not too surprising to see the potential
Warner Bros. sees in adapting the sci-fi action

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:24.940
flick for the small screen.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:29.410
The series could potentially retell the events
of the original film and implement's Liman's

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.660
idea for a sequel for a second season.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.009
Or, it could go in a different direction,
focusing on another military member getting

00:05:36.009 --> 00:05:37.430
stuck in a time loop.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:42.370
Anything is possible, especially if the series
hits HBO Max, which has proven itself as one

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:43.840
of the best streaming services.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.270
As for where the sequel currently stands:
who knows?

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:51.960
Only time will tell if "Edge of Tomorrow"
is destined to get a sequel or if the HBO

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.490
Max series will manifest.

